<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyrgi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyrgi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyrgi</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pyrgi was the chief port of the Etruscan city of Caere, on the Tyrrhenian coast at modern Santa Severa, north-west of Rome.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyrgi-tablets"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyrgi-tablets.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyrgi Tablets</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pyrgi Tablets are three gold plates of about 500 BCE, two inscribed in Etruscan and one in Phoenician, found in 1964 at ancient Pyrgi, on the Tyrrhenian coast of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhic-war"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyrrhic-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyrrhic War</span><span class="enc-type">War · 280–275 BCE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pyrrhic War was fought from 280 to 275 BCE between Rome and Pyrrhus, king of Epirus, who came to Italy to help the Greek city of Tarentum.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyrrho"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyrrho.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyrrho</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pyrrho of Elis was a Greek philosopher regarded as the founder of scepticism.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyrrhus-of-epirus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyrrhus of Epirus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pyrrhus was a king of the Molossians and of Epirus, sheltered as an infant by the Taulantian king Glaukias.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyrzyce"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyrzyce.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyrzyce</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pyrzyce, German Pyritz, is a town in the West Pomeranian Voivodeship of north-western Poland, south-east of Szczecin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pythagoras"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pythagoras.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pythagoras</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pythagoras was a Greek philosopher from Samos who, by tradition to escape the tyranny of Polycrates, settled at Croton in southern Italy around 530 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pythagoreio"><span class="enc-name">Pythagoreio</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pythagoreio is a small town on the south-eastern coast of Samos, Greece, on the site of the island's ancient capital.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pytheas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pytheas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pytheas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pytheas was a Greek navigator and geographer from Massalia, modern Marseille, who made a voyage to north-western Europe around 325 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pythia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pythia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pythia</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pythia was the priestess of Apollo at Delphi who spoke the god's oracles.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pythian-games"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pythian-games.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pythian Games</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pythian Games were the festival of Apollo at Delphi, one of the four Panhellenic games of ancient Greece.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pythodoris"><span class="enc-name">Pythodoris</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pythodoris was queen of Pontus, a granddaughter of Mark Antony and the wife of Polemon I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyu-ancient-cities"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyu-ancient-cities.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyu Ancient Cities</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pyu Ancient Cities are three walled cities of the Pyu people in the Irrawaddy basin of Myanmar, Halin, Beikthano and Sri Ksetra, inscribed as a World Heritage Site…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyxis-of-al-mughira"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyxis-of-al-mughira.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyxis of al-Mughira</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pyxis of al-Mughira is a carved ivory box made in 968 at Madinat al-Zahra for al-Mughira, a younger son of the caliph Abd al-Rahman III.</span></a></li></ul>
